"""Original applied lessons for existing GSC impression-earning owners."""
LESSONS=[
('/blog/vacation-rental-interior-designer-sarasota/','Build a Sarasota vacation-rental design brief',[
('Match the property to the stay','Start with the actual property rather than a generic coastal theme. Record whether guests arrive from a beach day, a family outing or a longer work stay, then map where bags, footwear, towels and food go. Inspect the entry, route to bedrooms, laundry location and outdoor transitions together. A decorative coastal palette does not solve a missing place for wet gear or a cramped group-meal setup.'),
('Test materials at the property','Bring finish and fabric samples into the rooms at different times of day. Compare glare, visible marks, care requirements and the cleaning team’s permitted methods. Record outdoor exposure and storage arrangements before selecting exterior pieces. Ask the proposed designer to explain why each high-touch material fits the operating routine rather than describing it only as coastal or durable.'),
('Worked brief: a family beach stay','For a hypothetical two-bedroom condo, the owner keeps the dining table but needs better arrival storage and bedroom lighting. The brief includes measured storage locations, towel-drying arrangements, furniture to retain, a room-by-room spending limit and the owner’s planned installation window. It asks the designer for an entry solution and a tested night-lighting plan before optional decor. This makes two proposals comparable without assuming a particular nightly-rate increase.'),
('What to bring to the design consultation','Provide a measured plan, room photographs, retained-item dimensions, care concerns and the expected guest capacity. Identify building access restrictions and any applicable property approvals with the responsible parties before scheduling deliveries. Ask who verifies dimensions, coordinates receiving and signs off on the finished rooms. Compare the complete scope, including local work left to you, rather than choosing by the design fee alone.')],['/learn/wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan/','/learn/rental-bedroom-nighttime-commissioning/','/markets/sarasota-fl.html']),
('/blog/diy-airbnb-makeover-before-after-strategies/','Plan an Airbnb makeover around guest problems',[
('Separate evidence from preference','Walk the rental as a guest and record what fails: inadequate usable seating, hard-to-find switches, missing storage, or surfaces that take too long to reset. Keep those observations separate from personal dislikes about the style. Review existing guest feedback for repeated issues and inspect the actual condition of the furniture before deciding to replace it.'),
('Use a keep, repair, replace sequence','For each room, mark what can remain, what needs a specific repair and what requires replacement. Measure retained items so new pieces coordinate with their scale and use. Start with essential guest functions and assign a person and completion date to each task. If a needed replacement is unavailable, approve an alternative against the same dimensions and care requirements rather than buying a visually similar piece on impulse.'),
('Worked phase: living-room refresh','A hypothetical host keeps a serviceable sofa, replaces an unstable coffee table and adds reading light before repainting. The first phase resolves the guest-use problems. The second phase tests paint and a limited set of accessories around the retained sofa. Photographs are updated after the room is complete. The example is a spending sequence, not an estimate of revenue improvement.'),
('Protect the reopening date','Work backward from a realistic acceptance walkthrough and photography date. Allow time for delivery inspection, assembly, cleaning and corrections. Decide which rooms can be refreshed independently and which require the property to be empty. A completed before-and-after photograph should represent the finished rental that guests receive, including usable lighting and the agreed furniture inventory.')],['/resources/guides/keep-repair-replace-audit/','/learn/furnishing-project-critical-path/','/blog/airbnb-photography-guide.html']),
('/blog/best-lighting-for-airbnb/','Test vacation-rental lighting by guest task',[
('Specify the task before the fixture','List the activities in each room: arriving with luggage, preparing food, reading in bed, using a mirror, working at a desk and finding the bathroom at night. A central fixture may illuminate the room without adequately supporting all of those tasks. Map the controls and the light needed at the actual use location before choosing decorative fixtures.'),
('Commission after dark','Test the installed room after sunset using the furniture arrangement guests will receive. Sit in each reading and dining position, locate switches from the doorway, and check for glare from the normal eye position. Record the task, fixture, control and observed problem. Correct one variable at a time so you can tell whether a lamp position, shade, source or control change helped.'),
('Worked test: a bedroom pair','In a hypothetical twin bedroom, both beds have lamps but one guest must cross the room to switch off the ceiling light. The owner documents that control problem and discusses an appropriate solution with the responsible installer. The acceptance test repeats the arrival-to-bed routine from both sleeping positions. A symmetric photograph would not have revealed the unequal experience.'),
('Create an operating record','Keep the exact replaceable light-source details, fixture instructions and any control setup with the room inventory. Follow applicable manufacturer instructions and use qualified professionals for electrical work. Ask the cleaner to identify a failed or mismatched light during reset, and retain a clear replacement specification so the room does not accumulate inconsistent light over time.')],['/resources/guides/layered-room-lighting-test/','/learn/rental-bedroom-nighttime-commissioning/','/learn/reference/lighting/']),
('/blog/tropical-color-palette-guide.html','Build a tropical palette that works in the actual room',[
('Choose a hierarchy','Select a background, a supporting material family and a limited accent direction. The background may come from existing flooring or large retained furniture rather than new paint. Test how the accents connect the room without repeating every tropical reference at once. A consistent hierarchy helps the room feel intentional while leaving functional items easy to locate.'),
('Evaluate samples in context','View paint, fabric and finish samples beside the actual flooring, window treatments and furniture. Compare morning, afternoon and artificial light. Note which surfaces look glossy, which colors become visually dominant and which materials show marks. Evaluate the permitted care process at the same time; a successful palette also has to survive the turnover routine.'),
('Worked palette: retained dark flooring','A hypothetical rental has dark flooring and a usable tan sofa. The owner tests a light wall background, natural-texture storage and a small green accent instead of replacing the sofa to copy an inspiration image. A sample board includes all three next to the flooring. The owner then tests the board in the living room and adjacent dining space so the transition is deliberate.'),
('Carry the palette into photographs','Photograph a small sample arrangement in the room before ordering the full accessory set. Check whether the accent overwhelms the guest-use surfaces or competes with the view. Use actual completed room photographs in the listing. Keep product and finish identifiers with the replacement list so a later refresh can preserve the palette without relying on memory or an old screenshot.')],['/learn/reference/identity/','/resources/damage-resistant-materials/','/blog/airbnb-photography-guide.html']),
('/blog/airbnb-home-office-design.html','Commission an Airbnb workspace for a real work session',[
('Define what you advertise','Decide what a guest can actually do at the workspace. Record the usable surface, chair, lighting, accessible power, connectivity and privacy conditions. A console styled with a closed laptop may not function as a desk when a guest opens the computer, places a drink and needs to take notes. Photograph the complete arrangement with the chair in its usable position.'),
('Run a seated task test','Sit at the desk and complete a realistic work task with a computer and charging cable. Check reach, screen glare, chair movement and whether the arrangement blocks circulation. Test the connection at that location using the property’s normal network configuration. Describe observed conditions accurately instead of promising that every guest device will achieve a particular speed.'),
('Worked tradeoff: studio workspace','A hypothetical studio has room for a compact desk only beside a window. The owner tests glare and chair pull-out space before buying. If the work position blocks the route to the bed, change the layout or select a different solution. Keeping a photographically appealing desk is less useful than providing a workable shared room.'),
('Leave a clear reset and support plan','Provide a simple place for cables and instructions for the guest-facing connection. Keep private owner equipment and records separate from guest use. Ask the cleaning team to verify that the desk is clear, the light works and the chair returns to the intended position. Update listing descriptions if the work setup changes so the photographs and written feature claim remain consistent.')],['/learn/studio-rental-zoning-without-extra-furniture/','/learn/reference/amenities/','/blog/guest-wifi-network-setup.html']),
('/blog/best-dining-table-for-airbnb/','Choose an Airbnb dining table by usable capacity',[
('Start with the full seating arrangement','Guest capacity should be tested with the actual chairs, table legs and adjacent circulation. A table’s nominal seating count does not establish comfort or access in your room. Place chair positions on the measured plan, account for the way chairs move during use, and check nearby doors, cabinets and routes. Consider how children, older guests and group meals use the arrangement.'),
('Test the tabletop as a meal surface','Set out the intended place settings and a realistic serving arrangement. Check whether table supports interfere with seated positions and whether the surface can be maintained using the allowed care method. If the table extends, test the extended configuration and document where the leaves and spare chairs are stored. The cleaning team should be able to reset the same arrangement consistently.'),
('Worked choice: six guests in a narrow room','A hypothetical six-person rental can fit a longer table but loses the route to the patio when the end chair is occupied. The owner compares a revised placement with a different table shape using the actual chair positions. The selection follows the meal and circulation test rather than the widest product photograph.'),
('Specify replacement and maintenance','Record the finish, product dimensions, chair variants, care instructions and replacement options. Inspect the delivered table for condition and stability, follow its assembly instructions and address any defects before guest use. Keep an approved substitute specification so a damaged piece can be replaced without restarting the whole room design.')],['/resources/guides/guest-capacity-furnishing-matrix/','/learn/kitchen-inventory-group-meal-test/','/learn/reference/dining/']),
('/blog/airbnb-photography-guide.html','Prepare design evidence before the listing photoshoot',[
('Show the room guests will receive','Finish the installation and operating checks before treating the room as ready for photography. Remove packaging, close the punch list and confirm the advertised furniture and amenities are present. Temporary styling should not imply a feature or capacity that disappears after the shoot. A clear image helps guests understand the layout and the actual experience.'),
('Plan useful views','Create a shot list from guest decisions: sleeping arrangements, dining capacity, shared spaces, workspace and outdoor access. Use a wide context view and detail views where they explain a real feature. Ask the photographer how to represent scale and connections between rooms accurately. Avoid an emphasis on decorative closeups that leaves the floor plan difficult to understand.'),
('Worked correction: lounge capacity','A hypothetical listing highlights a stylish sofa in one frame and extra chairs elsewhere. The owner checks whether those chairs are actually usable together, then photographs the agreed group arrangement. The caption states the real feature without implying additional sleeping or dining capacity. That revision improves clarity without manufacturing a new amenity.'),
('Keep a change log','Record the shoot date, room arrangement and features represented. When major furniture, workspaces or outdoor areas change, identify which photographs and captions need revision. Retain original property evidence and distinguish real photographs from design concepts or renderings. If you show a concept during planning, label it as such and do not present it as the completed guest space.')],['/learn/reference/identity/','/resources/guides/guest-capacity-furnishing-matrix/','/learn/rental-accessibility-feature-evidence/']),
('/blog/airbnb-kitchen-essentials-checklist.html','Size the kitchen inventory around a group meal',[
('Follow preparation, serving and cleanup','Build the checklist around how guests use the kitchen rather than collecting a long list of gadgets. Walk through food preparation, cooking, serving, eating, washing and storage. Verify that the room has suitable surfaces and that tools can be found and put away. Match the inventory to the advertised guest capacity and the way the home is positioned.'),
('Test the actual storage','Lay out the intended utensils, tableware and cookware, then place them in the assigned cabinets and drawers. Check appliance clearance and manufacturer instructions for the intended setup. Avoid filling every storage location so completely that guests cannot identify or return items. Keep owner supplies and replacement stock clearly separated from the guest inventory.'),
('Worked meal test: a group dinner','For a hypothetical eight-person rental, set out eight complete place settings and the serving pieces for one simple dinner. Walk through the preparation and cleanup using the available counters, cookware and dishwasher arrangement. Record shortages or awkward storage before ordering duplicate specialty gadgets. The exercise tests the complete meal rather than assuming the shopping list is sufficient.'),
('Make the checklist maintainable','Record quantities, locations, product details and replacement priorities. Ask cleaners to check critical items and obvious damage during turnover, with a process for reporting shortages. Keep the printable checklist alongside the room inventory so the owner can revise it when capacity or appliances change. Use manufacturer care and operating instructions for the actual products.')],['/learn/kitchen-inventory-group-meal-test/','/resources/guides/guest-capacity-furnishing-matrix/','/learn/reference/kitchen/'])]
